## Conclusion

This paper has shown that the Russian Federation has developed a comprehensive, multi-level system of support for small business, rooted in Federal Law No. 209-FZ and encompassing financial, property, informational, consulting, and educational instruments, coordinated through the national project and the Federal Corporation for SME Development.

The analysis of the Khabarovsk Territory demonstrates that the region actively implements this federal framework while adapting it to local conditions: its economy's industrial and export orientation, proximity to Asia-Pacific markets, and the special regimes of the Far East create distinctive opportunities for entrepreneurs. A functioning infrastructure — the "My Business" center, guarantee and microfinance funds, subsidies, and TOR benefits — provides real avenues for obtaining support.

At the same time, persistent problems remain: bureaucratic complexity of applications, compensatory mechanisms inaccessible to start-ups, insufficient awareness among entrepreneurs, and the geographic challenges of a vast region. Improving the effectiveness of the support system therefore requires continued simplification of procedures, digitalization of services, and proactive informing of the business community. Ultimately, the success of state support in the Khabarovsk Territory depends on continuous dialogue between the state and entrepreneurs to ensure that the measures offered are accessible, relevant, and effectively implemented.

